  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how plastic debris, especially microplastics, moves from land to the ocean and accumulates across Arctic or coastal marine environments. It shows multiple sources on land—traffic, agriculture, domestic wastewater, industry, and atmospheric deposition—feeding plastics into rivers and directly into coastal waters. Additional marine sources, such as ships and offshore platforms, contribute debris at sea. Arrows indicate transport pathways from the atmosphere, along rivers, across the sea surface, into the water column, and eventually down to sediments and the seafloor.\n\nThe image emphasizes that plastics are distributed through many environmental compartments rather than remaining only at the surface. Microplastics are depicted in seawater, sediments, sea ice, and snow, while larger plastic debris is highlighted at the sea surface, in sea ice, throughout the water column, and on the seafloor. The numerical ranges suggest strong variation in concentration among compartments, with especially high values appearing in snow, sea ice, and sediments. The figure also indicates ecological consequences by showing plastics entering the food web through marine organisms. Overall, it presents a connected source-to-sink system in which plastics are transported widely and stored in both marine and cryospheric reservoirs.",
